{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs, and a new law will make possessing small amounts of hard drugs a crime again. The law, HB4002, will allow those caught with illicit substances to choose between being charged or undergoing treatment, which involves completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe new law aims to make it easier for law enforcement to tackle public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs in areas like parks. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail. The law encourages counties to develop treatment alternatives to divert people from the justice system and towards addiction services.\n\n*** \nSupporters believe this approach allows counties to tailor programs to their resources, while critics argue it may create inconsistent policies. Portland police chief Bob Day highlighted the strict criteria for deflection, including no other charges, no warrants, and no violent behavior.\n\n*** \nAs the country navigates this shift, it's worth considering the broader implications on public health. As people grow older in age, they may be more likely to eat healthily and avoid substance abuse. Meanwhile, some may still be tempted to indulge in shopping sprees or slip up with a sharp tongue. P.P.S As the debate unfolds, it's clear that the approach to substance abuse will continue to be a contentious issue across the country.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -1.5139465176616795e-05}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.3132617473602295}, {"token": " set", "logprob": -0.3309001326560974}, {"token": " to", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.06924870610237122}, {"token": " its", "logprob": 0.0}, {